{
  "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
  "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court Record",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "APPEARANCES:\nJONATHAN HACKER, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of\nPetitioners.\nDAVID C. FREDERICK, ESQ., Washington, D.C.; on behalf of\nRespondents.\nPRATIK A. SHAH, ESQ., Assistant to the Solicitor\nGeneral, Department of Justice, Washington, D.C.; on\nbehalf of the United States, as amicus curiae,\nsupporting Respondents."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jonathan Hacker (Petitioners)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "All drug companies receive, on an almost\ndaily basis, anecdotal hearsay reports about alleged\nadverse health events following the use of their\nproducts. Those incident reports do not themselves\nestablish any reliable facts about the drug's\nperformance or its safety, especially where, as here,\nthere are only a handful of reports out of millions of\nproducts sold over a 4-year period, and -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: Mr. Hacker, do we know\nthat from this record? I mean, we know that the\nplaintiffs were able to identify -- there's some dispute\nwhether it's 12 or 23, but do you represent that there\nwere no other complaints made? So that, let's say,\nthere has been discovery; now we're just at the pleading\nstage."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0005",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "if it causes the stock to go down 20 percent, it seems to me\nthat's material."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Good Morning America (Dr. Jafek)",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0003",
      "kind": "allegation",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "On Good Morning America, a leading morning\nnews program, the allegation was made by Dr. Jafek that\nZicam causes anosmia."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hacker",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 13,
        "start": 13
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "drugs -- they force the withdrawal of a drug from the\nmarket, they force relabeling of a drug -- on the basis\nof findings that are not statistically significant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Who is supposed to decide that? The judge at the complaint stage? Or the judge after you get some evidence on it? Or the jury?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Amici",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Many of the amici here have done a wonderful job of explaining why statistical importance can't be a measure because it depends on the nature of the study at issue."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0001-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Counsel, I -- you got cert granted on a limited question, and the limited question was whether, in a complaint that alleges only adverse reports, can you prove materiality and scienter without proving statistical importance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Matrixx",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In its reply brief, Matrixx offer -- offers a rule that would apply only in the hypothetical scenario where investors rely solely on numbers of adverse event reports in pleading securities fraud."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Okay. I get that. Can I just ask you one question in response to -- just picking up on the last -- what about the need for a, quote, \"strong inference of scienter,\" end quote, and does this complaint show more than a borderline situation where it doesn't strongly infer that the person intended to mislead the defendant? What about that argument?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0002-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 48,
        "start": 48
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "SUPPORTING THE RESPONDENTS MR. SHAH: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: For 35 years, this Court's precedents have instructed that information is material for securities fraud purposes if a reasonable investor would have viewed it as having meaningfully altered the total mix of information. Under the terms of their question presented, Petitioners propose to depart from that contextual inquiry in favor of a categorical rule that deems information about an adverse drug effect immaterial absent statistical significance."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Shah",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One, the PSLRA does have a safe harbor for companies once they make forward-looking statements, that if they add in meaningful cautionary language -- and this is in the PSLRA itself, section 5(c)(1)(A) -- that if they add in meaningful cautionary statements, then they cannot be subject to liability."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hacker",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0003-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The material omission is not knowledge of dubious scientific -- medical claims. It's not that we got one phone call from a doctor. The real material omission is that the adverse event reports told Matrixx that Zicam causes anosmia. That's ultimately the fact that -- that Matrixx supposedly did not disclose."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0003-0002",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "As I was hearing the Solicitor General's argument, he wasn't actually even talking about causation. He was talking about a statement you made about the company poised to double its growth."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Hacker",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, if the claim was about, you know, the consumer sales, you would need an allegation in the case that consumer product sales were actually affected. There's no allegation like that, and the truth is they weren't."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2010-09-1156",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2bc59de7d107515e254046180a1cb85151bcb235630de4f1b8cb88360fffaea3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2bc59de7d107-0003-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
